course: dictionary
lesson: 13705
title: Firm demands and firm claims
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Insistence is a noun. It comes from the verb insist, which means to demand something firmly or to say something firmly and not give up. «At my mum's insistence, I wore a coat.» Mum kept asking, so the child wore the coat, even if they did not want to.
explain: Insistence can also mean saying firmly, again and again, that something is true. «Mia's insistence that the moon was made of cheese made everyone giggle.» She kept saying it firmly, even though it was not true.
explain: Look at the words next to insistence. At someone's insistence means because someone firmly asked or demanded. Insistence on means a firm demand for something. Insistence that means a firm claim that something is true.
fact: Insistence is a noun, so it names the act of insisting.
fact: «Insistence» means demanding something firmly and not giving up.
fact: «Insistence» can also mean saying firmly again and again that something is true.
fact: Common phrases are: at someone's insistence, insistence on, and insistence that.
fact: Insistence is usually uncountable, so we say «her insistence», not «her insistences».
exercise: «The teacher's insistence on silence made the room go quiet.» What does insistence mean here? | Loud noise | Happy surprise | Sleepy yawn | *Firm demand
exercise: «He went to bed early at his dad's insistence.» Why did he go to bed early? | *Because his dad firmly asked him to | Because he wanted to read | Because the bed was new | Because the lights went out
exercise: «Mia's insistence that the moon was made of cheese made everyone giggle.» What does insistence mean here? | A soft, quiet whisper | *Saying firmly again and again that something is true | Asking for a sandwich | Forgetting something important
test: «The club's insistence on fair play meant nobody could cheat.» What does insistence on mean here? | A dull speech about lunch | Forgetting the rules | *A firm demand for fair play | A quick game of tag
test: «Ella's insistence that she had locked the door was firm, but she was wrong.» What does insistence that mean here? | A rude joke about doors | *A firm claim that something is true | A happy wave to a friend | A quiet guess
exam: «Despite Leo's insistence, the coach did not change the team.» What does insistence mean here? | A new kit bag | *A firm, repeated demand that the coach ignored | A happy wave to the crowd | A sleepy yawn in the car
